---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { withBase } from '@lib/paths';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find((c) => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = withBase(`/tool/${category.segment}/${config.slug}/`);
const categoryHref = withBase(`/category/${category.slug}/`);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>Quick Answer <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Lorem ipsum is placeholder text: scrambled Latin-like words that fill a design before the real
      writing is ready. It lets you judge a layout, spacing, and typography without being distracted by
      what the words say. This tool generates it in your browser. Choose paragraphs, sentences, or words,
      set how many, and copy the plain-text result. Keep the familiar "Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet" opening
      or turn it off for fully varied filler.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open The Lorem Ipsum Generator →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="why-it-matters">
    <h2>Why It Matters <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      When you are designing a page, real words get in the way. If the layout contains a headline that
      says something interesting, everyone reviewing it starts reading and reacting to the message instead
      of the design. Empty boxes are no better, because they do not show how text will actually flow. You
      need something in between: text that looks real but says nothing.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is what lorem ipsum provides. It reads like ordinary prose, with a natural mix of short and long
      words, so a block of it fills a column the way real writing would. Because the words carry no meaning,
      the review stays focused on spacing, type size, line length, and rhythm. It is a simple idea that has
      kept designers and typesetters productive for a very long time.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea">
      <p>
        Placeholder text works precisely because it is meaningless. Its job is to look like content without
        being content, so the design can be judged on its own terms.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="how-it-works">
    <h2>How It Works <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The generator draws from a fixed pool of the traditional lorem ipsum words. You pick a unit,
      paragraphs, sentences, or words, and a count, and it builds the output to match. Sentences are given a
      natural, varied length and a capital first letter, with the occasional comma inserted, so the text
      reads like real prose rather than a flat list. Paragraphs group several sentences together.
    </p>
    <p>
      The variation comes from your browser's random source, so each generation produces a fresh block. The
      "start with Lorem ipsum" option pins the familiar opening phrase to the front, which many people
      expect to see; turning it off lets the whole block vary freely. Everything is built locally, so no
      request is made and nothing you generate leaves your device.
    </p>
    <p>
      The output is plain text. Paragraphs are separated by blank lines, and there is no HTML or styling
      attached. That keeps it easy to paste anywhere, from a design tool to a code editor, and to wrap in
      your own markup if you need paragraphs as HTML.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="examples">
    <h2>Examples <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A single paragraph, starting with the classic opening, reads like this:
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit. Sed do eiusmod tempor incididunt ut
      labore et dolore magna aliqua, enim ad minim veniam.</em>
    </p>
    <p>
      Set the unit to words and the count to eight, and you get a short run for a caption or a button label.
      Set it to sentences for a paragraph of a specific length. Set it to paragraphs and raise the count to
      fill a full article mockup. The character count under the output helps you land close to a target size
      when a space is tight.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="use-cases">
    <h2>Use Cases <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Placeholder text is a staple of design and development work:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Filling a mockup or wireframe before the copy is written.</li>
      <li>Testing how a layout handles long paragraphs and short ones.</li>
      <li>Demonstrating a template, theme, or component library.</li>
      <li>Checking a typeface, line height, and measure at a realistic length.</li>
      <li>Populating a content management system while building it.</li>
      <li>Creating sample data for a screenshot or a demo.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      In each case, the goal is to see the design working with text in place, then swap in the real content
      when it is ready.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="advantages">
    <h2>Advantages <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Lorem ipsum is fast, free, and familiar. It gives you realistic-looking text in a second, at any
      length, without writing a word. Because it is a long-standing convention, clients and teammates
      recognize it as placeholder and know not to read it as final copy. And it is not copyrighted, so it is
      safe to use in any project.
    </p>
    <p>
      Generating it here keeps the workflow simple and private. There is no account and no upload; you get
      the text and paste it wherever you need. The unit and count controls mean you can match the exact
      shape of the space you are filling, from a single label to a full page.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="limitations">
    <h2>Limitations <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Placeholder text can hide problems that real content would reveal. If a design assumes short headlines
      but the real ones run long, filler text will not show the break. For content-critical layouts, mock up
      with realistic copy instead, so the design is tested against what it will actually hold.
    </p>
    <p>
      Lorem ipsum is also, by design, meaningless, so it cannot help with anything that depends on the words
      themselves, such as reading level, tone, or search terms. And the most important limitation is
      procedural: never let placeholder text reach a published page. Filler that ships is a classic and
      embarrassing mistake.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake">
      <p>
        Shipping a page with lorem ipsum still in it happens more often than you would think. Do a final
        pass for placeholder text before anything goes live.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
  </section>

  <section id="privacy">
    <h2>Privacy <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Everything happens on your device. Generating text makes no network request and sends nothing to a
      server. There is no account and no tracking of what you produce. You can open your browser's developer
      tools, watch the network tab, and generate a block to confirm that nothing goes out.
    </p>
    <p>
      The tool remembers only your options, such as the unit and count, in local storage so the form looks
      familiar next time. It never saves the generated text, and the preference stays on your device.
      Clearing your site data removes it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="common-mistakes">
    <h2>Common Mistakes <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Leaving it in production.</strong> Placeholder text must be replaced before a page ships.
        Always do a final check.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Using it when the words matter.</strong> If real copy would change the design, mock up with
        realistic content instead.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Generating too much.</strong> Fill the space you have, not more. Extra text can make a layout
        look better than it will with real, shorter content.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Treating it as meaningful.</strong> The Latin-like words carry no message. Do not translate
        or interpret them.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="faq">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Short answers to the questions people ask most often, from where lorem ipsum comes from to whether you
      can generate words instead of paragraphs, are collected on the tool page itself.
    </p>
    <a href={`${toolHref}#faq`} class="cta-link">Read The Lorem Ipsum Generator FAQ →</a>
  </section>

  <section id="related-tools">
    <h2>Related Tools <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The lorem ipsum generator sits in the <a href={categoryHref}>Generators</a> category alongside the
      other creation tools. Where lorem ipsum fills a design with placeholder words, the other generators
      produce values software needs, such as passwords, tokens, and unique identifiers.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="internal-links">
    <h2>Where To Go Next <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href={toolHref}>Lorem Ipsum Generator</a> to create placeholder text now.</li>
      <li><a href={categoryHref}>Generators</a> to browse every generator on the site.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
